{"chapter": "formal-defos", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "06T0", "placeholders": {"STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:1b4675bcab0e1704eb15dd5abdaecaafd97954466fd75b2ec5b3a2ebdf495b05", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nMotivated by the preceding lemmas we make the following definition.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:915126aab886372f1067b227deb216c9cbafe04e58d1e0e0c0edc44a94b41bff", "unit_id": "tag:06T0:prose-0002"}
{"chapter": "formal-defos", "node_kind": "definition", "parent_tag": "06T0", "placeholders": {"FOOTNOTECLOSE_0001": "}", "FOOTNOTEOPEN_0001": "\\footnote{", "MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0002": "$\\xi$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0004": "$\\xi' \\to \\xi$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{section-miniversal-objects-existence}", "TEXTITCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITCLOSE_0002": "}", "TEXTITOPEN_0001": "{\\it ", "TEXTITOPEN_0002": "{\\it "}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-minimal-versal}\n", "suffix": "\\end{definition}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:d6fec86a70f3a79944ab9ed2c97926e5c48b3a66452655747f34fb22a0f93fd5", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:b564f5f33839b09f34dbf2485b46ba67935da2e3984014231811bec6f92e0ffd", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a predeformation category.\nWe say a versal formal object <MATH_0002> of <MATH_0003> is\n<TEXTITOPEN_0001>minimal<TEXTITCLOSE_0001><FOOTNOTEOPEN_0001>This may be nonstandard terminology. Many\nauthors tie this notion in with properties of tangent spaces.\nWe will make the link in\nSection <REF_0001>.<FOOTNOTECLOSE_0001>\nif for any morphism of formal objects\n<MATH_0004> the underlying map on rings is surjective.\nSometimes a minimal versal formal object is called <TEXTITOPEN_0002>miniversal<TEXTITCLOSE_0002>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:5c8369fa853494430fb934cbca41b014b1763fa965169332146b62e407f79de6", "unit_id": "tag:06T4:statement"}
{"chapter": "formal-defos", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "06T0", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:5407f3381e3c59fb1dafb63aa5ce9f34521fbfecefbfaecbd978d086d68efb5c", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:afa95d1af3a4d3e0467b489e0f805029841d23099ebaafb11635b2f4e3272df3", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nThe work in this section shows this definition is reasonable.\nFirst of all, the existence of a versal formal object implies that\n<MATH_0001> has (S1). Then the preceding lemmas\nshow there exists a minimal versal formal object. Finally, any\ntwo minimal versal formal objects are isomorphic. Here is a summary\nof our results (with detailed proofs).\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:13d84b2b8ce4d7a9d5b5d8c0746b2f1ba54642bf71a83a82d2a35761901c4713", "unit_id": "tag:06T0:prose-0003"}
{"chapter": "formal-defos", "node_kind": "lemma", "parent_tag": "06T0", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\begin{enumerate}", "STRUCT_0002": "\\item", "STRUCT_0003": "\\item", "STRUCT_0004": "\\item", "STRUCT_0005": "\\end{enumerate}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lemma-minimal-versal}\n", "suffix": "\\end{lemma}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:2dcb778ede4329b6e71133d05e099b677ddfe9365950cb12abe1674abb2ad6ed", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:41970103fb00ccbacefcfe8640209ef0cba7df239f5be61b38a82d6604f3bf0e", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a predeformation category which\nhas a versal formal object. Then\n<STRUCT_0001>\n<STRUCT_0002> <MATH_0002> has a minimal versal formal object,\n<STRUCT_0003> minimal versal objects are unique up to isomorphism, and\n<STRUCT_0004> any versal object is the pushforward of a minimal versal\nobject along a power series ring extension.\n<STRUCT_0005>\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:b7b5d421d3ea12a2a4dde589fa35549ca207de3e8f5932fe472e121b442cc211", "unit_id": "tag:06T5:statement"}
